  "answer": "To reduce the risk of reinforcement-learning agents irreversibly disrupting parts of their environment that are not represented in their reward functions, it has been proposed to fine-tune agents with an Attainable Utility Preservation (AUP) penalty using a latent auxiliary reward (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/5kurn5W62C5CpSWq6/avoiding-side-effects-in-complex-environments). The mechanism for this is a three-step training pipeline: first, the agent learns a 1-D auto-encoder of observations; second, it treats that latent dimension as an auxiliary reward to learn Q-values; and third, it derives the AUP penalty and trains its policy on the task reward minus the scaled penalty, thereby encouraging conservative behavior and preserving attainable utility for other goals (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/5kurn5W62C5CpSWq6/avoiding-side-effects-in-complex-environments)."
